from research_schema import F

F('trabeate-arcuate','Trabeate and arcuate construction in the Deccan','Trabeate construction spans between supports with horizontal members; arcuate construction uses arches and associated vault or dome systems. Hampi buildings can combine architectural vocabularies. The corpus distinguishes a visible post-and-beam hall from an arch-shaped decorative outline whose actual load path is not exposed.','The contrast is a structural vocabulary used in the course, not an exclusive religious ownership rule for all buildings.','hampi-pai',aliases='post-and-lintel|arcuate|trabeated')
F('cusped-arch','Cusped and foliated arches','A cusped arch has repeated lobes along its intrados or opening profile. The Hampi examples use such profiles within larger combinations of architectural forms. Identify the shape separately from the engineering: an ornamental arch can frame a load-bearing opening without itself revealing the complete construction.','A lobed outline establishes an ornament profile, not automatically the material or load-bearing technique.','hampi-government hampi-pai',aliases='foliated arch')
F('gopuram-shikhara','Entrance towers and sanctuary superstructures','The Hampi material differentiates prominent temple entrance towers from superstructures over other parts of a complex. Position and function must be read with form. A pyramidal outline reused on a courtly pavilion does not transform that pavilion into a temple gate.','Gopuram and shikhara should not be interchangeable labels for every tall or pyramidal roof.','asi-hampi unesco-hampi')
F('dome','Dome and monumental enclosure','At Gol Gumbaz the dome establishes the dominant mass above the tomb’s large enclosed volume. Exterior silhouette, the supporting arrangement shown in a diagram and interior acoustics provide different kinds of evidence about the same structure.','A dome is not the same element as a corner tower or minaret; distinguish spanning volume from vertical accent.','gol-government')
F('whispering-gallery','Whispering gallery at Gol Gumbaz','The district account identifies a whispering gallery associated with Gol Gumbaz. This is an acoustic property of an architectural space, not a separate monument. The source’s inconsistent numerical echo claims are not adopted here.','Do not memorize an exact echo count from this corpus; that number was not securely established.','gol-government')
F('iwan','Iwan and courtyard organization','An iwan is a vaulted space open on one side, often addressing a courtyard. The madrasa comparisons invite attention to the large framed opening, its depth and the court it serves, rather than simply treating it as a tall doorway.','An iwan is a spatial unit; a portal is an entrance treatment. Their forms can coincide without the terms being synonyms.','met-islamic-context')
F('mihrab','Mihrab and prayer orientation','A mihrab marks the prayer direction toward Mecca. Its niche can receive elaborate tilework or inscription. Identifying its directional role is distinct from describing the architectural frame or decorative technique.','A mihrab is not necessarily a functioning doorway, nor is every arched niche a mihrab.','met-mihrab')
F('ashurkhana','Ashurkhana and commemorative ritual','An ashurkhana is a space associated with mourning for Husayn and Karbala, with alams forming part of the commemorative setting. This ritual function differs from identifying a building solely by its arches or decorative tiles.','An ashurkhana should not be reduced to a generic mosque or described as a tomb solely because mourning occurs there.','ashurkhana-report')
F('chahar-bagh','Chahar bagh: quadripartite ordering','The Persian garden tradition combines organized divisions with water management and associations of paradise. The course considers related ordering at garden and urban scales. A grid-like city plan is a comparison to that model, not proof that every quadrant remained a planted garden.','Fourfold ordering, garden symbolism and a historically documented garden are distinct claims.','unesco-garden',aliases='charbagh|chaharbagh')
F('water-gardens','Water, gardens and stepped tanks','Water can structure both use and visual order: channels divide gardens, while stepped tanks expose changing levels through repeated terraces. UNESCO documents hydraulic systems at Hampi and water management in Persian gardens. These parallels do not establish one ritual or shared origin for every tank.','A water feature can have practical and ceremonial roles; a specific ritual requires evidence beyond proximity to a temple.','unesco-hampi unesco-garden')
F('relief-registers','Registers in architectural relief','Horizontal bands divide the Hampi royal relief programmes into successive fields of animals, people and courtly activity. Repetition helps organize a viewer’s movement along the monument. Each band must still be read for its own subject and preservation.','A continuous band need not be one continuous narrative event, and figures in different registers need not be contemporaneous.','hampi-dibba')
F('stucco','Stucco: surface ornament and evidence limits','The lecture captions identify stucco ornament on selected buildings; photographs show modeled surface detail and repeated projections. The reviewed material does not establish the plaster recipe, repair phases or exact workshop methods for these examples.','White or pale ornament is not proof that the whole building is made of stucco. Construction and surface treatment remain separate.',search='research03 research20',status='draft')
F('tilework','Tilework as architectural surface','Glazed tilework can organize colour, pattern and inscription on an architectural surface, as the documented mihrab example demonstrates. Motif, ceramic fabrication and building function should be described separately; a distant slide image cannot establish every firing or assembly method.','A tiled facade is not one painted ceramic slab, and a shared blue colour does not prove a common production centre.','met-mihrab')
F('circular-city','Circular city plans: comparison and limits','The Bijapur slide shows a rounded fortified perimeter, an inner citadel and an uneven network of routes. Its reference to Kakatiya precedents is interrogative. No retrieved source established direct descent of this particular plan from that precedent.','Similar rounded outlines do not prove a copied city plan or a mathematically perfect circle.',search='research24 research25',status='draft')
F('narasimha-vasantotsava','Narasimha, Vasantotsava and the limits of a lecture question','The Module 1 captions bring Narasimha, Chenchu Lakshmi, water and spring festival questions into the reading of the royal complex. The retrieved descriptions establish Mahanavami processions but do not settle the proposed connection for these particular panels and tank. Preserve the question as a question.','Mahanavami and Vasantotsava are not interchangeable labels, and the questioned link is not an established sole function of the platform.',search='research24',status='draft')
F('royal-devotion','Royal power and devotional imagery','The course juxtaposes courtly authority with sacred subjects, including ceremonial architecture and devotional images produced in court settings. Hampi’s royal and sacred complexes and the Sarasvati material provide grounded examples. Political and religious dimensions can coexist without every religious image being a royal commission.','Religious subject matter does not by itself identify a commissioner or prove that political significance is absent.','asi-hampi walters-sarasvati')
F('sacred-procession','Procession, ceremony and organized movement','The documented Mahanavami setting includes royal viewing of processions; relief bands represent horses, soldiers and courtly life. In each linked image, identify moving figures, attendants and the depicted focus before deciding whether the procession is royal, devotional or both.','A line of people is not sufficient to name a specific festival; the lecture label supplies only the identification it actually states.','hampi-dibba')
F('mangalore-tiles','Mangalore tiles, mission industry and contemporary art','Tallur’s installation uses roof tiles to connect local material with industrial and colonial histories. The Linden-Museum account links mission tile production to local clay and European technology. The Biennale record documents terracotta tiles and iron in a site-specific installation.','The history of tile manufacture is not the date of Tallur’s installation; a material can carry an earlier history into a contemporary work.','tallur-linden tallur-biennale')
